= Embodied cognition — Simple =

'''Embodied cognition''' is the idea that thinking is shaped by the kind of body an organism has and by how that body acts in the world.

For example, concepts about space, movement, tools, and actions may depend partly on systems used for seeing, moving, and interacting.

Embodied cognition does not mean the brain is unimportant.

It means a complete explanation of cognition may need the brain, body, action, and environment together.

There are several embodied-cognition theories, and they do not all agree about mental representations or how far cognition extends outside the brain.

